    "## Candidate models and validation-based selection\n",
    "Selection prioritizes phishing recall to reduce false negatives, while also considering phishing precision and F1. False negatives are phishing URLs incorrectly classified as safe. Raising recall often increases false positives (safe URLs flagged), so the validation results must be interpreted as a trade-off, not ranked by accuracy alone. Test data is not referenced in this section. KNN remains omitted because repeated neighbor-distance calculations over hundreds of thousands of sparse rows are impractical. Gradient Boosting is retained in a deferred candidate configuration, but excluded from the initial full evaluation: its previous grid adds 12 CV fits plus a refit, and its full-data cost should be assessed after the primary candidate set. It can later be benchmarked and evaluated with the same train-only CV and validation policy.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Fixed prediction policy\n",
    "No custom decision threshold is selected and no threshold tuning is performed. Validation and held-out test predictions both use the selected estimator pipeline's same default `predict()` policy. Estimators that expose probabilities generally predict using their class/probability decision (commonly the 0.5 boundary for binary classifiers); margin-based estimators use their decision boundary. LinearSVC is a margin-based classifier: its signed decision boundary is not a probability threshold. No historical SVC metrics are directly comparable because LinearSVC uses a different solver and default loss/intercept treatment. The evaluation reports the estimator defaults without interpreting scores as calibrated probabilities.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Candidate replacement note\n",
    "LinearSVC replaces `SVC(kernel=\"linear\")` as a distinct estimator. It uses a different optimization implementation and default loss/intercept treatment, so its results must be evaluated as a new candidate and not compared as if they were historical SVC results.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Full-dataset execution safeguards\n",
    "The next section uses all 507,194 usable rows and the fixed train/validation/test partitions; it must not silently substitute a sample. Full evaluation may require substantial time and memory, particularly for tree ensembles. The held-out test partition remains outside search and selection. Fold metrics, validation metrics, compatibility metadata and the final test result are stored as inspectable JSON under `evaluation_checkpoints/url_safety_seed_42`; fitted estimators and production model artifacts are never serialized. Each fold emits flushed progress, and completed compatible folds/validations are reused on restart. A dataset, split, candidate or preprocessing mismatch raises an error and requires a fresh checkpoint directory. The held-out test cell requires a finalized validation selection and reads its completion marker to avoid repeating an already completed test evaluation.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Active search spaces\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Logistic Regression:** `C` = 0.1, 1, 10; three log-spaced regularization strengths.\n",
    "- **LinearSVC:** `C` = 0.1, 1; a compact low/default-strength comparison for the linear margin model.\n",
    "- **Decision Tree:** `max_depth` = 10, 25, unlimited; compares shallow, deeper, and unrestricted trees without changing the existing range.\n",
    "- **Random Forest:** 100 trees with `max_depth` = 25 or unlimited; isolates the depth constraint while keeping ensemble size fixed.\n",
    "- **XGBoost:** 100 estimators, `learning_rate` = 0.1, `max_depth` = 3 or 6; a narrow depth comparison with the current learning rate and estimator count fixed.\n",
    "\n",
    "All active grids are evaluated with three-fold shuffled `StratifiedKFold` on training rows. Grid searches run sequentially (`n_jobs=1` at the search level); Random Forest and XGBoost retain their estimator-level `n_jobs=-1`, so those workers are not nested inside concurrent searches. The deferred Gradient Boosting configuration is retained at 100 estimators, `learning_rate` = 0.1 or 0.5, and `max_depth` = 2 or 3. Its four combinations would add 12 CV fits plus one refit; benchmark it separately on training data before adding it to the full run.\n",
    ""
